<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Finding the exponent with a larger base</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A number \(b\) is obtained by raising a base \(a\) to an unknown positive integer exponent \(n\):</p>

\(a^n=b\).

<p>For this question, the base is \(a={@a@}\) and the result is \(b={@b@}\).</p>

<p>Use the change-of-base formula</p>

\(n=\frac{\log(b)}{\log(a)}\)

<p>to determine the integer exponent \(n\).</p>

<p>Enter your answer as an integer.</p>

<p>[[input:ans1]]</p>
<p>[[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>

<p>Start with the given exponential equation:</p>

\(a^n=b\).

<p>Take logarithms of both sides:</p>

\(\log(a^n)=\log(b)\).

<p>By the power rule for logarithms,</p>

\(n\log(a)=\log(b)\).

<p>Rearrange the equation to obtain the exponent:</p>

\(n=\frac{\log(b)}{\log(a)}\).

<p>Substitute the values of \(a\) and \(b\):</p>

\(n=\frac{\log({@b@})}{\log({@a@})}={@n@}\).

<p>Hence, the correct exponent is \(n={@n@}\).</p>

<p>To verify the answer, substitute the exponent into the original equation:</p>

\({@a@}^{@n@}={@b@}\).

<p>The equation is satisfied, so the answer is correct.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
